                         <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web desi=
gn and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=
=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h=
3>Makin' Stuff</h3>=0D=0A<p>The most fun websites to build are websites tha=
t interact with outside sources. A user is an outside source! I=E2=80=99m o=
bviously a bit partial to a website that asks a user to build stuff. That=
=E2=80=99s fun. But any outside source can be fun. Accepting uploads and do=
ing something weird or useful with the data. Or using a third-party API for=
 interesting data.</p>=0D=0A<p>There is something magically fun about a wea=
ther app, for instance. You=E2=80=99re designing the site, but it=E2=80=99s=
 designed around data you don=E2=80=99t control. Your job is to present the=
 weather in a pleasing and useful way. That=E2=80=99s difficult to do even =
when you=E2=80=99re essentially copying patterns that already exist for it,=
 and extra difficult to do something novel that could attract actual custom=
ers.</p>=0D=0A<p>I=E2=80=99ve seen a college program that has students lite=
rally build a weather app. That=E2=80=99s a great project if you ask me. It=
 involves an outside source of data, so you learn how to do that and make i=
t efficient and resilient and respect bandwidth and limits and all that. Th=
e students also learn that websites are both front-end and back-end, and ne=
ither can fully compensate for weaknesses in the other.</p>=0D=0A<p>A weath=
er site is a pretty saturated space. But I believe in you, you can do it! T=
here are other opportunities out there, though. Perhaps after you=E2=80=99v=
e got your feet wet with interacting with outside sources, something else w=
ill click for you that feels more personal.</p>=0D=0A<p>I was thinking abou=
t this after reading Terence Eden=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://post.spmailt=
echnol.com/f/a/GdpCA7lHcMNIEGt4GeQDwg~~/AABEfhA~/o4_WMusjZuaBhb5fNUszQ3o3MD=
ebnls-1zTOdi8Xadea26dohwmXaqZZ9uvY5jWu80DcPfk4rFUBemBDKvxa9mfHos_yhKUOkoOAo=
tKT8qimvyIeex9Wk-OZ1EDL25h60o5WhVP7BnFFle4xGNLp-3QkhJ7Yq2knWOqet1fB1mQ~">Ar=
e there any open APIs left=3F</a>. It was very interesting to me as I was v=
aguely under the impression that most of the really interesting cool open A=
PIs didn=E2=80=99t really exist anymore. And, to be clear, =E2=80=9CAPI=E2=
=80=9D meaning general data access from some third-party source. A classic =
is that GitHub offers <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/nMafH0q=
pYNZJQ7zVSGr_lw~~/AABEfhA~/PPU4IcS6hN-uRJNtqho6_N404pQIa-dQeIQq__M20CD0psZo=
Fpj4bKF1eYeXWdj0oqYAlrbsbkdtNVYfopu4QMFE-bkpbXDbiw2afGR8epBFPIPGsaAdt1KNGyC=
ZZK0uOCS-fXC5IKx5i3-YTCgoiEhTPd_VZXxDvZ5VeLUyRC2slrgk95IUIYPKi7iUhkwEaOL4AL=
hihPyU4CQhYHmIqws0frfYPHD1il-LknAgi4gdSusatwUnUOCfuBlg06Bk">REST and GraphQ=
L APIs</a> for your use. Could you build something interesting with access =
to GitHub data=3F Or, perhaps a better question is could you build somethin=
g interesting with access to GitHub data <em>and whatever other outside sou=
rce you can think of</em>. Terence=E2=80=99s blog post lists some APIs stil=
l going strong.</p>=0D=0A<p>Speaking of outside sources, let me leave you w=
ith a somewhat random collection of links to tools and ideas that maybe-jus=
t-maybe could be alchemized into an interesting website.</p>=0D=0A<ul>=0D=
